      "code": "2.40(b)(2)",
      "kind": "Critical",
      "repeat": false,
      "desc": "Attending veterinarian and adequate veterinary care (dealers and exhibitors).",
      "narrative": "Two tapirs died of an accidental overdose of a copper supplement. On January 7, 2026, a male 16-year-old tapir named\n\u201cJohHi\u201d was found dead after an acute onset of blood in the urine. Three days later a female 18-year-old tapir named\n\u201cRinny\u201d also developed blood in the urine and emergency treatment was implemented by the veterinary team. Her\ncondition continued to decline, and she was euthanized on January 10, 2026. Cause of death was determined to be an\nincorrect dosage of copper supplementation despite repeated blood serum copper values being below normal. The\nincorrect dosage led to fatal copper poisoning. Improper calculation of feed additives can lead to animal illness, disease,\nand death.\nThe facility must use appropriate methods to prevent, control, diagnose, and treat disease and injury.\nThe facility implemented immediate corrective actions to strengthen safety protocols so there is better oversite of the\nsupplement administration process before the inspection on June 16, 2026.\nThis inspection and exit interview were conducted with facility representatives.\nAdditional Inspectors:\nKATHRYN VERNON, ANIMAL CARE INSPECTOR n"
    }
  ],
  "narrative": "2.40(b)(2)  Critical\n   \nAttending veterinarian and adequate veterinary care (dealers and exhibitors). \nTwo tapirs died of an accidental overdose of a copper supplement. On January 7, 2026, a male 16-year-old tapir named \n\u201cJohHi\u201d was found dead after an acute onset of blood in the urine. Three days later a female 18-year-old tapir named \n\u201cRinny\u201d also developed blood in the urine and emergency treatment was implemented by the veterinary team. Her \ncondition continued to decline, and she was euthanized on January 10, 2026. Cause of death was determined to be an \nincorrect dosage of copper supplementation despite repeated blood serum copper values being below normal. The \nincorrect dosage led to fatal copper poisoning. Improper calculation of feed additives can lead to animal illness, disease, \nand death. \nThe facility must use appropriate methods to prevent, control, diagnose, and treat disease and injury.  \nThe facility implemented immediate corrective actions to strengthen safety protocols so there is better oversite of the \nsupplement administration process before the inspection on June 16, 2026. \n \nThis inspection and exit interview were conducted with facility representatives. \n \nAdditional Inspectors: \nKATHRYN VERNON, ANIMAL CARE INSPECTOR n",
